{"entries":{"chapter-7-study-decision-making-and-advance-care-planning-what-matters-most|Chapter 7 Study: Decision Making and Advance Care Planning: What Matters Most":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Decisions should integrate medical evidence with the person's goals, preferences, function and life context (p109–111).":["u3"],"copy|Multiple conditions, atypical presentations and uncertain prognosis complicate disease-specific recommendations (p110).":["u4"],"copy|Ask who the patient wants involved in decisions, including a possible future surrogate (p111).":["u5"],"copy|Social circumstances, cognition, mood and practical function are part of the decision-making assessment (p111).":["u6"],"copy|Ask whether a test is accurate, tolerable and likely to change management toward outcomes the patient values (p112).":["u7"],"copy|Consider pretest probability, treatment availability, comorbidity and whether the patient would accept the proposed intervention (p112).":["u8"],"copy|A technically informative result may offer little benefit when the subsequent treatment does not fit the patient's goals (p112–113).":["u9"],"copy|Clinicians contribute medical expertise; patients contribute expertise about themselves. Shared decisions require a conversation about both (p113).":["u10"],"copy|Elicit priorities across longevity, comfort, cognition and physical function, rather than demanding isolated choices about every treatment (p113).":["u11"],"copy|Adapt the setting for sensory/cognitive impairment; use a quiet, well-lit, unhurried discussion and additional encounters when needed (p113).":["u12"],"copy|Explore prognosis and help patients identify feasible goals when their preferred outcome cannot be achieved (p113).":["u13"],"copy|ACP is an iterative process: explore values, identify a surrogate and translate preferences into care plans (p113).":["u14"],"copy|Document discussions through advance directives, clinical records or appropriate portable medical orders (p113).":["u15"],"copy|For serious life-limiting illness, including advanced frailty, voluntary discussion of life-sustaining treatment preferences may lead to a POLST form in U.S. settings where available (p113).":["u16"],"copy|Revisit planning across care settings and illness changes; use communication tools and account for family, cultural, spiritual and religious values (p114–115).":["u17"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u18"],"copy|CHAPTER 7s / HAZZARD":["u19"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 7; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u20"],"copy|Start with what matters":["u21"],"copy|Use tests purposefully":["u22"],"copy|Share the decision":["u23"],"copy|Advance care planning":["u24"],"copy|No past-exam questions are currently linked to this chapter.":["u25"]},"maxId":25}
